  "query_preview": "/Through Thursday/...\nIssued at 1254 PM MDT Thu Jul 23 2026\n\nScattered to numerous storms are expected across the area today. As\nof early this afternoon, dewpoints are in the 50s to mid 60s for \nmuch of the plains and portions of the mountains. PWs are still \nsitting near the 99th percentile to the climatological max for \nthis time of the year. Flow aloft is a bit faster today compared \nto yesterday, so storms will be moving quicker (which will help \nlower the flash flooding potential). However, since quite a few \nareas have seen rain the past few days, certain areas will have \nlower flash flood thresholds and will be more susceptible to flash\nflooding with any heavy rain that moves over. Along with the \nheavy rain and flash flooding threat, we'll also need to monitor \nstorms for strong winds associated with microbursts or strong \noutflows. Most areas will likely see around a half an inch or \nless, but there will be localized areas that see multiple inches \nof rain today.\n\nThe upper level ridge will start to build east over the area on \nFriday. The ridge will continue to build into the weekend, with the \ncenter of the high sitting near or over Colorado Saturday and \nSunday. As of the most recent model runs, the majority of \nensemble members of the GFS and Euro have temperatures at or above\n100 degrees for Denver on Sunday. Even warmer temperatures are \nexpected across the eastern plains on Sunday, with a few spots in \nthe northeastern plains sitting at a 30-50% chance of highs \nreaching 105. A Heat Advisory may be needed across portions of the\nUrban Corridor and NE plains for Sunday if the forecast holds.\n\nThe upper high will weaken slightly and shift a bit more east and \nsouth for early next week. This could be enough to let a few weak \ndisturbances move through the flow aloft. We could also start to \nsee an increase in moisture Sunday into early next week, helping \nbring rain chances back to the plains. Temperatures next week are\nstill a bit uncertain. Some models are holding onto the triple \ndigit temperatures through Monday, while others drop temperatures \na few degrees with the weakening of the thermal ridge and \nincreased precip chances. There will likely be a surface low near \nthe region next week, which could bring a weak front through the \narea. The impact of the front will be pretty minimal, but it \ncould drop temperatures closer to normal.",
